## sess125 — the owed worker is written; blocker 2 is code-complete

**Build: VERSION `0.11.441` → `0.11.442`, srcversion `40C1AA02000E86B0240C748` → `B2E4583BA40413F3B2704D2`. `make modules` exits 0, no errors, no new warnings. Still STOP-SHIP; fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #17) rather than starting at #1, same reason as sess119–124: the DLM core is mid-landing under the sess118 stop-ship ruling, and the publication half shipped without a collector is strictly worse than either end state.

**What changed.** The obligation state machine now has the guaranteed collector the sess121 ruling required. Sessions 122–124 built the publisher — every destructive clear writes down what it might leave behind *before* the I/O that would discharge it. Until today the only thing that ever read those records was an inline pass at the bottom of `lreq_finish`, and that had three independent failures: on a resource whose local attempts had all already left there was **no collector at all** (the leak this work exists to close); it charged an unbounded contended CAS loop to an XFS thread; and it got exactly one try, so a pass that lost every CAS achieved nothing and nothing came back.

`caw_owed_worker_fn` replaces all three. It owns a kthread, is woken by every event that can make an obligation runnable, and retries with exponential backoff indefinitely — it never abandons a record, because a leaked EX-waiter bit is what makes every peer defer fresh readers behind a request nobody is making. Escalation means shouting (P253) and, at teardown, refusing to exit quietly (P254).

Two structural choices worth flagging:

- **The sweep is claim-then-dispatch, not walk-and-dispatch.** The bucket chain cannot be walked across an unlocked dispatch because `lreq_gc` frees entries. One locked pass claims up to 16 entries into a stack array — setting `owed_busy` and raising `pin`, which is precisely what makes `lreq_gc` refuse them — and the pointers stay valid until release. The sweep start is left *at* the bucket where the quota ran out, so a partially consumed bucket resumes instead of waiting a full rotation.
- **A failed worker start fails the mount.** It is the only collector; mounting without it means mounting a filesystem that can permanently wedge its peers. The unwind joins `bast_poll_thread` on that path. Symmetrically, `stop` joins the worker *before* the BAST threads so the teardown drain's slot I/O runs while the transport is still up — bounded at 2s.

`lreq_finish` lost both of its old jobs: the CLRWAIT park and the inline owed run (the sess116 `e=NULL` corruption path). It is now bookkeeping plus one wake edge — an attempt's departure is what turns a refused plan permissive. The dead header constants and counters went with it.

**What is not done: the review.** This is a new kthread doing destructive CAW I/O, and each of the last four rulings found real defects in the preceding session's landing. Per RULE 5 the next step is a GPT consult before any rig cycle, with four specific questions recorded in ccmemory `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess125-owed-worker-LANDED-blocker2-complete` — chiefly whether `-ENOENT` from `find_slot` is genuinely terminal under a concurrent peer claim, and whether the split between the dispatch's `gen0` (used only for the terminal retraction) and `caw_drop_own_waiter`'s own internally-merged gen can let a terminal retraction erase an intent published in between. After that, closure still requires the sess113 debugfs exerciser — a green board cannot close this defect, since sess111 measured the reconcile arm entered 0 times on all 32 nodes.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling. The marker stays unwritten.

